  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".

  "Licensees" and "recipients" may be individuals or organizations.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a way requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a "work based on" the earlier work.

  A "covered work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

  To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you liable for infringement under applicable
copyright law, except executing it on a computer or accessing its private
copy.  Propagation includes copying, distribution (with or without
modification), public performance, and in some cases other activities.

  To "convey" a work means to propagate that enables other parties to
make or receive copies.  A pure server interaction is not a conveyance.

  An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
to the extent that it includes a convenient and prominently visible
feature that alerts the user to the fact that there is no warranty (except
where warranties are provided), that licensees may convey the work under
this License, and how to access a copy of this License.  An interactive
user interface also displays these types of information.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it.  "Object code" refers to any non-source
form of a work.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for that purpose, one of the following: (1) the
official standard defined by the IEEE 1003.1-2001 standard, available at
any usual place where IEEE standards are made available; (2) the POSIX
interface, available at any usual place where IEEE standards are made
available; or (3) the open standard network services interface (INADDR),
as an informal guide only.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) enters into the production of that
work, and (b) serves only to link with or interface with the work.

  A work that uses only numerical parameters, data structure layouts and
accessors, and small macros and small inline functions (ten lines or less
in length), is a means of linking with the work.

  The "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, execute, and to modify the work,
including scripts to control those operations.  However, it does not
include the work's System Libraries, or general-purpose tools or generally
available free programs which are used unmodified in association with the
work, but which are not part of the work.  For example, Corresponding
Source includes interface definition files associated with source files
for the work, and the source code for shared libraries and dynamically
linked subprograms that the work specifically requires by name, as an
intimate functional necessity of the using the work.

  Corresponding Source also includes any encryption, authorization, and
how to access it.

  When code is conveyed as object code in a form that disallows access to
install and execute modified versions by users (a form of access control,
commonly called "TPP" or similar), the right to possess, use, modify and
propagate the work is denied unless the licensee respectfully requests
and is granted access as provided in section 6.

  Notwithstanding the above, the Corresponding Source need not include
anything that users can regenerate automatically from other Corresponding
Source (such as dependency lists or build tools).

  Corresponding Source for a work in source code form is that same work.

